window.SI_PAPERS = [{"id":0,"title":"Planetary Information Processing and Memory Storage Through Deep Time: A Bounded Order-of-Magnitude Reconstruction from Prebiotic Chemistry to the Modern Technosphere","plain_title":"Counting a planet's memory and computing","thesis":"Count everything on Earth that stores or processes information, from the first cells to today's data centers, keep each kind in its own units, and four findings stand out.","summary":"The paper builds a census of Earth at 64 moments over 4.1 billion years. At each moment it estimates ten separate quantities: three kinds of DNA storage, two kinds of memory, three kinds of event rate (cell chemistry, nerve signals, chip operations), total power, and how long information persists. Today's values are built from published counts of cells, animals and devices, with uncertainty ranges from 10,000 random draws. Older values are scaled back from those anchors using fossils and rock chemistry.","claims":[{"claim":"Life and technology together use a fixed, countable amount of power.","section":"Abstract, finding 1; Table B1","paper_value":"65.0 TW for life plus 19.5 TW for technology, 84.5 TW in all","data_value":"65.0 TW, 19.5 TW and 84.5 TW","source":"Power ledger (paper0_power_closure_ledger.csv): compartments added up","verdict":"matches","note":"The six compartments in the ledger sum to the stated totals."},{"claim":"Life uses about half of what plants capture from sunlight.","section":"Abstract, finding 1","paper_value":"52% of 125 TW","data_value":"52.0% (65.0 ÷ 125)","source":"Census value for life's power; the 125 TW figure is cited from outside literature","verdict":"matches","note":"The division is right; the 125 TW anchor itself is not in the project's data."},{"claim":"Microbial power divides into deep-underground, standby and active cells.","section":"Abstract, finding 1","paper_value":"0.69 TW, 3.89 TW and 9.42 TW, totalling 14.00 TW (corrected 9 Nov 2026; first version said 0.69 GW and 10.11 TW)","data_value":"0.69 TW, 3.89 TW and 9.42 TW, totalling 14.00 TW","source":"Power ledger, rows 1a to 1c","verdict":"matches","note":"Corrected on 9 November 2026: the abstract, section 2.4 and the appendix now give the ledger's values, which add up to 14.00 TW."},{"claim":"The number of microbial cells follows from their total carbon.","section":"Abstract, finding 1; section 2.1","paper_value":"3.10×10³⁰ cells from 77.5 billion tonnes of carbon at 25 femtograms per cell","data_value":"3.10×10³⁰ cells","source":"Census: microbial carbon divided by carbon per cell","verdict":"matches","note":"77.5×10¹⁵ g ÷ 25×10⁻¹⁵ g gives 3.1×10³⁰."},{"claim":"Cell chemistry is the largest event rate on the planet.","section":"Abstract, finding 1","paper_value":"2.80×10³³ events a second, 95% range 9.46×10³² to 8.25×10³³ (corrected 9 Nov 2026)","data_value":"2.80×10³³ events a second, 95% range 9.5×10³² to 8.2×10³³","source":"Census and the uncertainty ledger (paper0_monte_carlo_summary.csv)","verdict":"matches","note":"Corrected on 9 November 2026: the abstract now quotes the range from the paper's own uncertainty table. That table's central value, 2.70×10³³, is slightly below the census's 2.80×10³³."},{"claim":"All the DNA in living cells can be counted in bits.","section":"Abstract, finding 1","paper_value":"3.60×10³⁷ bits in total, 1.84×10³⁷ under selection, 3.80×10¹² unique","data_value":"3.60×10³⁷, 1.84×10³⁷ and 3.80×10¹² bits","source":"Census, today's row","verdict":"matches","note":"All three values are in the census as stated."},{"claim":"Every animal nervous system together fires a countable number of signals.","section":"Abstract, finding 2","paper_value":"3.43×10²⁴ neurons, 3.00×10²⁷ synapses, 3.00×10²⁶ signals a second, 95% range 9.08×10²⁵ to 9.92×10²⁶ (corrected 9 Nov 2026)","data_value":"3.43×10²⁴ neurons, 3.00×10²⁷ synapses, 3.00×10²⁶ signals a second (95% range 0.9×10²⁶ to 9.9×10²⁶)","source":"Census; synapses × 0.10 signals a second each; uncertainty ledger","verdict":"matches","note":"Corrected on 9 November 2026: the range now comes from the uncertainty table; the earlier 1.5 to 8.5×10²⁶ is kept and labelled as a firing-rate band."},{"claim":"Two mass extinctions each removed more than nine tenths of nerve signalling.","section":"Abstract, finding 2; section 4.2","paper_value":"94% at the end of the Permian, 92.5% at the asteroid impact","data_value":"94.0% and 92.5%; 95% ranges 87.8 to 99% and 84.6 to 98.5%","source":"Census: change between the moments either side of each extinction; uncertainty ledger","verdict":"matches","note":"Both drops recompute exactly from the census. They are constrained scenarios, not measurements."},{"claim":"All the world's chips together execute a countable number of operations.","section":"Abstract, finding 3; Table D1","paper_value":"1.89×10²² operations a second, 9.00×10²¹ of them on AI accelerators, drawing 80 GW","data_value":"1.89×10²² operations a second, 9.00×10²¹ on AI accelerators, 80 GW","source":"Device ledger (paper0_device_inventory_ledger.csv)","verdict":"matches","note":"Totals match, and 15 million accelerators × 10¹⁵ operations × 60% use gives 9.0×10²¹. Corrected on 9 November 2026: the abstract now splits the rest the way the device ledger does."},{"claim":"Chips remain far below nervous systems when everything is added up.","section":"Abstract, finding 3","paper_value":"about 15,900 times below all animals and 65 times below all human brains","data_value":"15,900 times and 65 times","source":"Census: nerve signals ÷ chip operations; 8.2 billion people × 1.5×10¹⁴ signals each ÷ chip operations","verdict":"matches","note":"Both ratios recompute. The census's own population figure is 8.1 billion, which gives 64."},{"claim":"One large AI cluster out-runs one human brain.","section":"Abstract, finding 3","paper_value":"3.00×10²⁰ operations against 1.50×10¹⁴ signals a second: 2 million times","data_value":"2.0 million times","source":"Census: largest single system today ÷ one human brain","verdict":"matches","note":"The division is right. It compares chip operations with nerve signals, which are different kinds of event."},{"claim":"Modern storage holds a great deal but would not last long unpowered.","section":"Abstract, finding 4; Table P1","paper_value":"1.20×10²³ bits installed; 9.96×10²³ bit-years unpowered; mean lifetimes 3.5 years flash, 7.0 hard disks, 25 tape; 20 GW of refresh power, 9 GW of it for storage (corrected 9 Nov 2026)","data_value":"Memory ledger: the same values. The census row for 2026 carries 4.2×10²³ bit-years instead of 9.96×10²³.","source":"Memory ledger (paper0_memory_persistence_ledger.csv) and the census","verdict":"differs","note":"Corrected on 9 November 2026: lifetimes, labels and the split of the 20 GW now follow the memory ledger. One difference remains, in the project's data rather than the paper: the census and the ledger disagree on unpowered persistence."},{"claim":"The reconstruction is calibrated on a large catalog of systems.","section":"Abstract, opening","paper_value":"950 systems, 2,616 measurements, 1,275 sources","data_value":"975 systems, 2,641 measurements, 1,300 sources","source":"Catalog workbook: distinct ids counted on the Systems, Measurements and Sources sheets","verdict":"differs","note":"The catalog has grown by 25 systems, 25 measurements and 25 sources since the paper was written. The paper's counts describe the catalog it used, so they were left as they are."}],"rationale":[{"step":"Keep ten quantities in their own units and never merge them into one score.","assessment":"holds","note":"The paper does this throughout; bits, events a second, watts and bit-years are never added together."},{"step":"Build today's values bottom-up from published counts and require the pieces to add up.","assessment":"holds","note":"Carbon to cells to power closes exactly in the ledgers, and the totals recompute."},{"step":"Scale back through deep time from today's anchors using fossils, rock chemistry and living relatives.","assessment":"weak","note":"There is no other way to do it, and the paper labels these moments as scenarios, but values before the modern inventory cannot be tested and carry wide ranges."},{"step":"Compare cell chemistry, nerve signals and chip operations by counting events a second.","assessment":"weak","note":"The counts are sound, but one reaction, one nerve signal and one chip operation are not equal amounts of work, as the paper itself says. The ratios say how often, not how much thinking."},{"step":"Carry uncertainty through with 10,000 random draws.","assessment":"holds","note":"The ledger has the ranges, though the abstract quotes narrower ones for two quantities."}],"conclusions":[{"conclusion":"The world's chips have not overtaken the world's nervous systems.","assessment":"supported","note":"The joint 95% range of the chip-to-nerve ratio runs from 1.6×10⁻⁵ to 2.3×10⁻⁴, so it stays far below 1 across the whole range."},{"conclusion":"One frontier AI cluster exceeds one human brain by six orders of magnitude.","assessment":"partly","note":"The arithmetic is right. Read as raw event counts it holds; read as a statement about capability it does not follow, because the events differ."},{"conclusion":"Mass extinctions acted as resets that cleared small-brained incumbents and released the rise of mammals and birds.","assessment":"partly","note":"The size of the drops is a model scenario with wide ranges, and the paper does not test the causal step from clearing to release."},{"conclusion":"Digital storage needs continuous power to avoid being erased.","assessment":"supported","note":"After the 9 November correction the paper says that of the 20 GW refresh power, 9 GW keeps storage from being erased and 11 GW refreshes working memory, which matches its ledger."}],"inconsistencies":["Section 2.4 gives each group of microbes its own carbon per cell and event rate (for example 100 transitions a second for deep microbes), while Table B1 uses a uniform 25 femtograms of carbon per cell and different rates. Both give the same totals.","The uncertainty table's central values (2.70×10³³, 3.02×10²⁶, 1.87×10²²) differ slightly from the census (2.80×10³³, 3.00×10²⁶, 1.89×10²²).","The census and the memory ledger give different unpowered persistence for outside memory (4.2×10²³ against 9.96×10²³ bit-years); the paper follows the ledger.","The world population is 8.2 billion in the text and 8.1 billion in the census.","Figures 1 to 3 were drawn before the correction and still label the 1.5 to 8.5×10²⁶ band as the uncertainty of nerve signalling."],"bottom_line":"After the 9 November correction, today’s totals, the ratios between them, the uncertainty ranges and the extinction drops all agree with the paper’s ledgers and can be relied on as order-of-magnitude estimates. The deep-time values and the causal story about extinctions remain scenarios, and five smaller differences between the text, its tables and the project’s data are left for the authors.","revised":"On 9 November 2026, after this check, six errors in the abstract and body (a wrong unit, a value that broke the power budget, two uncertainty ranges, a device breakdown, memory lifetimes and the use of 20 GW of refresh power) were corrected to match the paper’s own tables. The original is kept as a backup.","pdf":"papers/paper0.pdf","corrected_docx":"papers/paper0-corrected.docx","pdf_stale":false},{"id":1,"title":"Physical Constraints on Latency and Energy Use in Biological and Artificial Information Processing","plain_title":"How fast and how cheaply can brains and chips compute?","thesis":"Brains and computers are both held back by the same physics, how far a signal must travel and how much heat it makes, and when you compare like with like a brain synapse is only about 71 times more energy efficient than a top graphics chip, not 100,000 times.","summary":"The paper builds a table of 33 computing systems, from a single brain synapse and a squid nerve to Nvidia graphics chips, light-based chips and superconducting logic cooled to 4.2 kelvin, and records for each one how long a signal takes, how much power it uses and how many operations it gets per joule. It argues that signal delay is set by distance divided by speed (or distance squared for diffusion), that the famous Landauer heat limit does not affect single decisions, and that the energy gap between brains and chips is much smaller than usually claimed. It then adds side arguments about how brain energy use scales with neuron count and how brain mass scales with body mass. We recomputed the key ratios from the project's data files and the paper's own stated inputs.","claims":[{"claim":"A cortical synapse is about 71 times more energy efficient than a dense 16-bit operation on an Nvidia H100 chip.","section":"Abstract; 4.4","paper_value":"10¹⁴ events per joule divided by 1.41×10¹² operations per joule = 70.9 times","data_value":"70.9 times (1.0×10¹⁴ / 1.41×10¹² from the hardware file)","source":"cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv, synapse row divided by H100 dense row; the synapse value itself is taken from Attwell and Laughlin (2001), not measured here","verdict":"matches","note":"We divided the two rows and got 70.92; the arithmetic is right, but both inputs are literature numbers copied into the file, and a synaptic event and a 16-bit multiply are different kinds of operation, which the paper itself admits."},{"claim":"Superconducting AQFP logic still beats everything after paying a roughly 400-to-1 electricity penalty for cooling to 4.2 kelvin.","section":"Abstract; 4.4; Supplementary Note S2","paper_value":"7.14×10¹⁹ operations per joule at the core, 1.79×10¹⁷ after the 400 times cooling penalty; 1,790 times better than a synapse","data_value":"Carnot coefficient 0.0142, ideal cost 70.4 W per W, at 17.5% of ideal 402 W per W; 7.14×10¹⁹ / 402 = 1.78×10¹⁷; 1.79×10¹⁷ / 10¹⁴ = 1,790","source":"Recomputed the Carnot chain from 4.2 K and 300 K and the ratios from cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv rows for AQFP core, AQFP wall-plug and synapse","verdict":"matches","note":"Every step of the cooling-penalty arithmetic reproduces within rounding; the 1.4×10⁻²⁰ joules per switch is a single literature value for a laboratory circuit, not a working computer."},{"claim":"Cooled AQFP logic is five orders of magnitude (about 120,000 times) more efficient than the best room-temperature CMOS chip.","section":"Abstract; 4.4","paper_value":"1.2×10⁵ times","data_value":"127,000 times against the H100 only; 11,800 times against the Apple M4 as printed in Table 1 (1.52×10¹³), 23,600 times against the M4 in the data file (7.6×10¹²), 39,800 times against the B200","source":"cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv: 1.79×10¹⁷ divided by each CMOS row's efficiency","verdict":"differs","note":"The factor 1.2×10⁵ only appears if the H100 (1.41×10¹² ops per joule) is called the best CMOS chip, but the paper's own table lists three CMOS chips that beat it, and against the best of them the gap is about 10⁴, four orders of magnitude, not five."},{"claim":"Table 1 reports the measured latency, power and efficiency of the electric fish pacemaker, the dragonfly interception neuron and the Apple M4 Neural Engine.","section":"4.2; Table 1","paper_value":"Fish: 1 mm, 0.16 microseconds, 10⁻⁸ W, 5×10¹⁰ ops per joule. Dragonfly: 12 mm, 4.5 m/s, 28 ms, 5×10⁻⁷ W, 8×10⁸ ops per joule. M4: 2.5 W, 2.11×10⁻¹⁰ s, 1.52×10¹³ ops per joule (15.2 TOPS/W)","data_value":"Fish: 2 mm, 1 millisecond, 2×10⁻⁷ W, 5×10⁹ ops per joule. Dragonfly: 4 mm, 4 m/s, 25 ms, 5×10⁻⁶ W, 8×10⁶ ops per joule. M4: 5 W, 4.44×10⁻¹⁰ s, 7.6×10¹² ops per joule (7.6 TOPS/W)","source":"cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv, the three rows for these systems, compared field by field with Table 1","verdict":"differs","note":"All three rows disagree with the data file: the fish latency differs 6,000-fold, the dragonfly efficiency 100-fold and the M4 efficiency 2-fold (the paper's 15.2 TOPS/W is 3.8×10¹³ ops per second divided by 2.5 W, while the file uses 5 W); the other 21 printed rows match the file."},{"claim":"Brain mass grows with body mass to the power 0.87 across the cleaned species catalog.","section":"5.2","paper_value":"slope 0.8727, intercept −1.8751, R² 0.7362, standard error 0.0376","data_value":"277 rows, slope 0.7955, intercept −1.963, R² 0.576, standard error 0.041 (all rows with positive masses); slope 0.83, R² 0.80 if the obvious non-animals are dropped","source":"cleaned_biological_species.csv, ordinary least squares of log10 brain mass on log10 body mass","verdict":"not_reproducible","note":"No subset we tried gives 0.8727 or R² 0.7362; the file has 277 rows, not the 195 species the authors' review notes describe, and it still contains entries such as Wikipedia, a starling flock and a penguin huddle with 'brain mass' larger than 'body mass'."},{"claim":"Brain power grows with neuron count to the power 0.86 rather than following the 0.75 body-mass law.","section":"Abstract; 3.4.2","paper_value":"exponent 0.86 ± 0.03, R² 0.941","data_value":"not in the species file (it has no power column); the only project file with both brain power and neuron counts gives exponent 0.98 ± 0.05, R² 0.93 over 29 rows","source":"cleaned_biological_species.csv has no brain power column; neural_sensory_evolution_timeseries.csv, log10 brain watts against log10 neurons","verdict":"not_reproducible","note":"The paper says the exponent comes from 'our calibrated biological species dataset', but that dataset has no power numbers, and the one project table that does gives a slope near 1.0, not 0.86."},{"claim":"A budget of 500 picowatts per neuron predicts a human brain power of 18 to 22 watts.","section":"3.4.2","paper_value":"8.6×10¹⁰ neurons × 5.0×10⁻¹⁰ W = 18 to 22 W","data_value":"8.6×10¹⁰ × 5.0×10⁻¹⁰ = 43 W","source":"Arithmetic on the paper's own inputs","verdict":"not_reproducible","note":"Multiplying the paper's two numbers gives 43 W, about double the 20 W it claims to match; the 0.86 exponent is not applied in the sentence and no prefactor is given that would bring it down."},{"claim":"The 33 systems span 16 orders of magnitude in latency and 12 orders of magnitude in energy efficiency.","section":"Abstract","paper_value":"16 orders (latency), 12 orders (efficiency)","data_value":"14.8 orders in latency (2.13×10⁻¹¹ s to 1.36×10⁴ s) and 14.2 orders in efficiency (4.35×10⁵ to 7.14×10¹⁹ ops per joule)","source":"cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv, min and max of total latency and of efficiency across all 33 rows","verdict":"differs","note":"Neither span matches the file; the 12-order efficiency span only appears if you use the 24 rows printed in Table 1, which omit the least efficient system (the blowfly reflex at 4.35×10⁵ ops per joule)."},{"claim":"Neurotransmitter diffusion across a 20-nanometre synaptic gap takes about 200 microseconds.","section":"2.2; Figure 1","paper_value":"about 200 microseconds, from L = 20 nm and D = 0.76 µm² per ms","data_value":"L² / 2D = (2×10⁻⁸ m)² / (2 × 7.6×10⁻¹⁰ m²/s) = 0.26 microseconds; the hardware file's synapse row nevertheless lists 200 microseconds of transport time","source":"Paper's own diffusion formula with the paper's own inputs; cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv synapse row","verdict":"not_reproducible","note":"The paper's formula and inputs give a quarter of a microsecond, about 760 times shorter than stated; 200 microseconds is roughly the whole synaptic delay (vesicle release plus receptor response), not the diffusion time, and Figure 1 plots the wrong value."},{"claim":"Surface bacteria use about 1 femtowatt per cell, 1,000 times more than deep-sediment bacteria at 1 attowatt, and this gap follows from first principles.","section":"Abstract; 3.4.4","paper_value":"10⁻¹⁵ W per cell versus 10⁻¹⁸ W per cell, a 1,000 times gap; surface value derived as 1.0×10⁻⁹ J divided by 1,800 s plus motility","data_value":"1.0×10⁻⁹ J / 1,800 s = 5.6×10⁻¹³ W, about 560 times larger than the stated 10⁻¹⁵ W; the deep-sediment sum (1.2×10⁻¹⁹ + 8.8×10⁻¹⁹ = 1.0×10⁻¹⁸ W) does reproduce","source":"Arithmetic on the paper's own inputs; the project's hardware file lists the E. coli chemotaxis cluster at 10⁻¹⁵ W","verdict":"not_reproducible","note":"The derivation's first term alone is 560 times bigger than its own answer, so the 'first-principles' 1,000-fold gap does not follow from the numbers shown (they would give roughly 560,000-fold); the 1 femtowatt and 1 attowatt figures are literature values, not results of the derivation."}],"rationale":[{"step":"A signal cannot arrive faster than distance divided by speed (or distance squared over diffusion constant for diffusing molecules), so the slowest chain of steps sets the minimum decision time.","assessment":"holds","note":"This is standard physics and the hardware file is built on it, but the paper's own examples break it: the electric fish latency of 0.16 microseconds in Table 1 is 300 times shorter than the 50 microseconds its 1 mm path at 20 m/s allows, and the synaptic-gap diffusion time is overstated 760-fold."},{"step":"The Landauer heat cost of erasing a bit is irrelevant to how fast one decision can be made; it only limits how fast you can keep cycling once heat must be removed.","assessment":"holds","note":"We reproduce 2.871×10⁻²¹ J at 300 K and 1.44×10⁻²² s for a 20 W brain; the argument is sound, though the text's 'twelve orders of magnitude below a millisecond' should be nineteen."},{"step":"Compare like with like: an on-chip 16-bit operation against a synaptic event gives a gap of about 71 times, so the usual 100,000-fold gap is an artifact of comparing synapses with slow off-chip memory fetches.","assessment":"weak","note":"The 71 reproduces, but both inputs are copied literature values, the two operations are admitted to be different things, and the paper's own 'artifact' comparison gives 20,000, not 100,000, so the claimed origin of the famous gap is not actually demonstrated."},{"step":"Superconducting logic cooled to 4.2 kelvin is so efficient that even after a 400-fold electricity cost for refrigeration it beats both synapses and CMOS chips.","assessment":"weak","note":"The cooling arithmetic is right and the 1,790-fold lead over a synapse follows, but the 'five orders of magnitude over the best CMOS' only holds against the H100; against the best CMOS chip in the paper's own table it is about four orders."},{"step":"Brain energy follows neuron count (exponent 0.86), not the 0.75 body-mass law, and brain mass follows body mass with exponent 0.87 in the cleaned catalog.","assessment":"unsupported","note":"Neither regression reproduces from the project files (we get 0.98 for power versus neurons and 0.80 for brain versus body with R² 0.58), and the worked human example gives 43 W, not the 18 to 22 W claimed."}],"conclusions":[{"conclusion":"When you compare a dense on-chip operation with a synaptic event, the brain's energy advantage over a modern GPU is about 71 times, not 100,000 times.","assessment":"partly","note":"The ratio is correctly computed from two literature values, but the paper does not show that the 100,000 figure comes from memory fetches (its own version of that comparison gives 20,000), and it never settles whether a synaptic event and a 16-bit multiply are comparable units."},{"conclusion":"Cryogenic superconducting logic, even charged 400 times for cooling, beats synapses by about 1,800 times and room-temperature CMOS by 100,000 times.","assessment":"partly","note":"The synapse comparison and the cooling penalty hold; the CMOS comparison is overstated by about a factor of ten because it uses the H100 rather than the more efficient CMOS chips in the same table."},{"conclusion":"Decision latency is set by signal travel distance and speed, with a square-law penalty only for diffusion, and the Landauer heat limit plays no role in single-decision speed.","assessment":"supported","note":"The physics and the Landauer arithmetic check out; the weak spots are specific example numbers (fish pacemaker, synaptic gap, Trichoplax) that disagree with the very formula being illustrated."},{"conclusion":"Brain power scales with neuron count to the 0.86 power and brain mass with body mass to the 0.87 power, so Kleiber's 0.75 law does not govern brains.","assessment":"not_supported","note":"Neither exponent can be recovered from the project data, the species file is still contaminated with non-animals, the human worked example is off by a factor of two, and the paper itself defers the proper analysis to a future Paper 2."}],"inconsistencies":["Three rows of Table 1 (electric fish pacemaker, dragonfly neuron, Apple M4) disagree with the hardware data file by factors of 2 to 6,000, while the paper says the table was built from that file.","The electric fish entry gives a latency of 0.16 microseconds for a 1 mm path at 20 m/s, which is 300 times faster than the paper's own rule 'time is at least distance divided by speed' allows; 0.16 microseconds is the timing jitter reported in the source, not a latency.","The paper says the best room-temperature CMOS chip is beaten by 1.2×10⁵, using the H100 at 1.41×10¹² ops per joule, yet its own Table 1 lists the Apple M4 at 1.52×10¹³, the B200 at 4.5×10¹² and Cerebras at 3.23×10¹², all more efficient than the H100.","The abstract says the 100,000-fold gap is an artifact of comparing synapses with DRAM fetches, but the paper's own synapse-to-DRAM ratio is 20,000, five times smaller.","The abstract claims 16 orders of magnitude in latency and 12 in efficiency; the data file gives 14.8 and 14.2.","The synaptic-gap diffusion time is stated as 200 microseconds and plotted that way in Figure 1, but the paper's own formula with its own inputs gives 0.26 microseconds.","The 500 picowatt per neuron budget times 86 billion neurons is 43 W, not the 18 to 22 W the text says it predicts.","The surface-bacterium derivation's first term (10⁻⁹ J over 1,800 s) is 5.6×10⁻¹³ W, 560 times larger than the 10⁻¹⁵ W it is said to yield, so the 'derived' 1,000-fold gap does not follow.","The brain-body regression (slope 0.8727, R² 0.7362) does not reproduce from the cleaned species file (slope 0.80, R² 0.58, 277 rows), and the authors' own review notes say the file had 195 species.","The brain-power exponent 0.86 is attributed to the species dataset, which contains no power measurements at all.","The introduction says vacuum tubes switched six orders of magnitude faster than neurons and then quotes 10⁻⁶ s versus 10⁻³ s, which is three orders.","Section 3.2 says 1.44×10⁻²² s is twelve orders of magnitude below a 1 millisecond membrane time constant; the gap is nineteen orders.","Trichoplax is said to coordinate over 1 mm at 150 micrometres per second in 1.5 s; 1 mm divided by 150 micrometres per second is 6.7 s, and quoting a steady speed contradicts calling it pure diffusion.","The brain's share of body energy is said to span 'over three orders of magnitude' from 0.0008 to 0.25, which is a factor of 312, about 2.5 orders.","Songbird neuron density is given as 1.56×10⁸ per gram in Section 3.4.1 and 2.4×10⁸ per gram in Section 5.4.","The catalog is described throughout as 950 systems, 2,616 measurements and 1,275 sources, but the spreadsheet in the data folder holds 975 systems, 2,641 observation ids and about 1,300 sources.","The catalog numbers the paper attaches to the squid giant axon, the blue whale and modern humans point to a roundworm, an extinct small-bodied human species and no entry at all in the project's species file.","Table 1 is introduced as covering the 33 benchmark substrates but prints 24 rows, and Figure 1 plots systems (a transatlantic internet ping, historical vacuum-tube computers) that are not among the 33 in the data file."],"bottom_line":"A reader can rely on the core physics (signal delay is distance over speed, Landauer heat does not limit single decisions) and on the headline arithmetic: synapse over H100 is 71, cooled superconducting logic over synapse is about 1,800. Do not yet rely on the 'five orders over the best CMOS' claim (it is about four), on any of the brain-scaling exponents (neither reproduces from the project data), or on several worked examples whose own numbers do not multiply out.","pdf":"papers/paper1.pdf","corrected_docx":"","pdf_stale":false},{"id":2,"title":"Macroevolutionary Allometry of Brain Architecture: Resolving the Encephalization Paradox via Clade-Specific Cellular Scaling and Deep-Time Grade Shifts","plain_title":"Why small bird brains can out-think big mammal brains","thesis":"Brain size alone misleads: what matters is how many neurons a brain packs in, and birds, primates and other mammals each follow their own packing rule.","summary":"The paper takes a table of 54 animals (worms to whales to fossil humans) with their brain mass split into cortex, cerebellum and the rest, plus neuron counts for each part. It fits straight lines on log-log plots to show that one universal brain-to-body rule fails, and that neurons per gram of cortex scale differently in primates, in other mammals and in birds. From that it argues that a raven's 14.5 g brain holds more neurons than a pig's much larger brain, proposes a conversion factor of about 2.8 to put bird and primate brains on one scale, and claims human ancestors gained neurons 21 times faster than the vertebrate average. We recomputed every number we could from the project's two data files and re-ran the fitting script.","claims":[{"claim":"The common raven packs an extremely high number of neurons per gram into its forebrain (pallium).","section":"Abstract and 4.1","paper_value":"2.35×10⁸ neurons per gram of pallium (and 1.8–3.2×10⁸ for corvids and parrots generally)","data_value":"1.22×10⁸ neurons per gram (1.20×10⁹ pallial neurons divided by 9.8 g); crow 1.35×10⁸, parrot 1.56×10⁸","source":"cns_structural_evolution.csv: pallial neurons divided by pallial mass for the raven, New Caledonian crow and African grey parrot","verdict":"differs","note":"The data gives about half the stated raven density, and no bird in the file reaches the 1.8×10⁸ lower bound the paper quotes; the figure's own corvid points sit near 1.2–1.6×10⁸ while its label says about 2×10⁸."},{"claim":"A raven has more than twice as many neurons as a domestic pig despite a brain nearly nine times smaller.","section":"Abstract and 4.2","paper_value":"Pig brain 125 g with 1.05×10⁹ neurons (3.0×10⁸ cortical); raven 2.17×10⁹, so more than 2 times the pig","data_value":"Pig brain 180 g with 1.5×10⁹ neurons (3.1×10⁸ cortical); raven has 1.45 times the pig's neurons, brain 12 times smaller","source":"cns_structural_evolution.csv rows for Sus scrofa domesticus and Corvus corax; also cleaned_biological_species.csv (pig rows give 180 g/1.5×10⁹ and 125 g/2.5×10⁹)","verdict":"differs","note":"The pair 125 g and 1.05×10⁹ appears in neither data file; with the project's numbers the raven has 45 percent more neurons than the pig, not more than double, though the four-fold pallial advantage (1.2×10⁹ versus 3.1×10⁸) does hold."},{"claim":"In primates and fossil humans, cortical neuron count rises with cortex mass along a steep, tight line, keeping neuron density nearly constant.","section":"4.1 and Abstract","paper_value":"slope 0.7727, intercept 7.6481, R² 0.9843; density 1.4–5.1×10⁷ per gram across four orders of magnitude","data_value":"slope 0.7727, intercept 7.8642, R² 0.9843 (15 taxa); density 1.35×10⁷ to 5.10×10⁷ per gram; cortex mass spans 4.8 g to 1300 g (2.4 orders of magnitude)","source":"cns_structural_evolution.csv: least-squares fit of log10(cortex neurons) on log10(cortex mass) for the 15 primate and hominin rows","verdict":"matches","note":"Slope and R² reproduce exactly, but the intercept in the text is wrong (7.65 versus 7.86), the range is 2.4 not 4 orders of magnitude, and a slope of 0.77 means density falls almost four-fold across primates, so 'near-linear' and 'nearly constant density' overstate it."},{"claim":"In non-primate mammals neuron count rises more slowly than cortex mass, so neurons per gram collapse in big brains, bottoming out in the killer whale.","section":"4.1 and Abstract","paper_value":"slope 0.6471, intercept 7.4215, R² 0.9646; density from 8.3×10⁷ per gram in shrews down to 2.4×10⁶ in killer whales, 2.7×10⁶ in sperm whales","data_value":"slope 0.6471, intercept 7.4879, R² 0.9646 (16 taxa); killer whale 2.42×10⁶, sperm whale 2.79×10⁶; lowest value is the blue whale at 7.8×10⁵, then beluga 1.24×10⁶ and elephant 1.97×10⁶; no shrew in the file (mouse is the top at 8.06×10⁷)","source":"cns_structural_evolution.csv: fit on the 16 non-primate mammal rows and the cortical density column","verdict":"matches","note":"Slope and R² reproduce; the intercept differs, the 'shrew' value is not in the data, and the killer whale is not the low end since three other mammals in the same file are packed even more loosely."},{"claim":"Bird pallium follows its own 'hyperdense' line with a slope above 1, meaning bigger bird brains get denser.","section":"4.1 and Abstract","paper_value":"slope 1.1600, intercept 8.0123, R² 0.9231","data_value":"slope 1.1600 ± 0.167, intercept 7.9391, R² 0.9231 from only 6 birds; Figure 2 panel D instead shows slope 1.62, R² 0.82","source":"cns_structural_evolution.csv: fit on the 6 bird rows (chicken, pigeon, zebra finch, parrot, crow, raven)","verdict":"matches","note":"The slope reproduces, but with six points its standard error is 0.17 so a slope of 1 is within reach, the text intercept is wrong, and the manuscript's own figure quotes different slopes (1.62 for birds, 0.78 for primates, 0.55 for other mammals) for the same relationship."},{"claim":"Pooling all species into one brain-versus-body line gives a mediocre fit that hides the clade differences.","section":"3.1","paper_value":"202 species; slope 0.8641, intercept -1.8750, R² 0.7327, AIC 139.8","data_value":"277 rows; slope 0.7955, intercept -1.963, R² 0.5764; the file also holds synthetic computers, 'mutualism' systems and single organs, not just species","source":"cleaned_biological_species.csv: least-squares fit of log10(brain mass) on log10(body mass) for all rows; the paper's own macros file and Figure 1 give 0.80 and 0.58","verdict":"differs","note":"The text's slope, R², species count and intercept are all stale; the project's data, its macros file and its Figure 1 agree with each other (0.80, R² 0.58, 277 rows) and disagree with the text."},{"claim":"A statistical test decisively prefers separate lines per clade over one universal line.","section":"3.1 and Abstract","paper_value":"AIC difference 260.7 (anything above 10 counts as decisive)","data_value":"The macros file says 473.0; re-running the script's recipe gives 473.0, but it compares a fit on 277 rows of one file with fits on 54 taxa of another file. A fair comparison on the same 43 vertebrates gives a difference of about 44","source":"scripts/compile_paper2_pgls.py re-run on cleaned_biological_species.csv and cns_structural_evolution.csv","verdict":"not_reproducible","note":"Neither 260.7 nor the method behind 473.0 survives: AIC values can only be compared for models fitted to the same data, so the published number is meaningless, though a like-for-like test still favours separate clade lines (difference about 44)."},{"claim":"A conversion factor lets you multiply a bird's pallium mass to place it on the primate neuron-count curve.","section":"4.3.4","paper_value":"factor 2.84 ± 0.31, from bird pallial density 2.15×10⁸ per gram and primate cortical density 7.18×10⁷ per gram","data_value":"Arithmetic from the stated inputs gives 2.84, but the inputs are not what the data holds: corvid/parrot mean density 1.38×10⁸, primate mean 2.1×10⁷ (3.1×10⁷ for living primates only); the data's ratio is 5.7 (or 3.9)","source":"cns_structural_evolution.csv: mean cortical density and cortex mass fraction for the raven, crow and parrot versus the 15 primate and hominin rows","verdict":"differs","note":"The paper's primate density of 7.18×10⁷ is more than double any primate in its own table (the highest is the marmoset at 5.1×10⁷) and contradicts section 4.1's own range of 1.4–5.1×10⁷, so the factor 2.84 cannot come from this dataset."},{"claim":"The African elephant keeps almost all of its neurons in the cerebellum, with a cortex smaller in neuron count than a macaque's or chimpanzee's.","section":"5","paper_value":"97.5 percent of 257 billion neurons (250.7 billion) in the cerebellum; cortex 5.6 billion, fewer than a rhesus macaque or a chimpanzee (6.2 billion)","data_value":"250.7/257 = 97.5 percent; cerebellar density 2.70×10⁸ per gram; elephant cortex 5.6 billion versus macaque 1.71 billion and chimpanzee 6.2 billion","source":"cns_structural_evolution.csv elephant, macaque and chimpanzee rows; also the mouse, macaque and human rows of the table in section 5","verdict":"matches","note":"The elephant figures and all four cerebellum table rows recompute correctly (mouse row uses slightly different masses from the file), but the elephant cortex has more than three times the macaque's 1.71 billion cortical neurons, so 'fewer than a rhesus macaque' is false by the paper's own data."},{"claim":"Human ancestors gained neurons about 21 times faster than the long-run vertebrate background rate.","section":"6 and Abstract","paper_value":"background 0.01538 per million years (1.5×10⁶ to 2.7×10⁸ neurons over 340 Myr); hominin 0.32416 per million years (3.05×10¹⁰ to 8.606×10¹⁰ over 3.2 Myr); ratio 21.1","data_value":"hominin rate 0.32416 reproduces; background with the stated 2.7×10⁸ gives 0.01527 (ratio 21.2), and 0.01538 only appears if the endpoint is the file's 2.8×10⁸ platypus value; the start value 3.05×10¹⁰ for Australopithecus afarensis is marked 'imputed' in the file","source":"cns_structural_evolution.csv: lamprey, platypus, Australopithecus afarensis and Homo sapiens rows, natural-log difference divided by time span","verdict":"matches","note":"The arithmetic holds within rounding, but the 'background' endpoint is a living platypus standing in for a 160-million-year-old ancestor, the hominin start value is an imputed guess, and a 3-million-year window will almost always beat a 340-million-year average (the step from Ardipithecus to afarensis alone gives 0.39 per Myr, 25 times background)."}],"rationale":[{"step":"One brain-versus-body line for all animals fits poorly; separate lines for primates, other mammals, birds and fish/reptiles fit much better, so evolution moved in distinct 'grades'.","assessment":"weak","note":"Separate lines do fit better (a fair test gives an AIC gap of about 44), but the published pooled numbers are stale, the published AIC gap compares models fitted to two different data files, the 'phylogenetic' correction (lambda 0.912) is a hard-coded constant and the grade slopes are plain least squares, and two groups have only six animals each."},{"step":"Neurons per gram of cortex follow different rules in primates (slope 0.77), other mammals (0.65) and birds (1.16), so equal brain mass does not mean equal neuron count.","assessment":"holds","note":"The three slopes and R² values reproduce exactly from the 54-taxon file and the ordering is clear, though intercepts in the text are wrong, the bird line rests on six points, and the paper's own Figure 2 quotes different slopes."},{"step":"Therefore a raven's small brain can hold more neurons than a pig's large brain and match a capuchin monkey, which explains why corvids behave as cleverly as monkeys.","assessment":"weak","note":"The direction is right for pallial neurons (raven 1.2×10⁹ versus pig 3.1×10⁸), but the pig numbers in the text match no data file, the raven's total is 45 percent above the pig's rather than double, the project's capuchin has 3.7×10⁹ neurons versus the raven's 2.17×10⁹, and intelligence is asserted from citations rather than measured here."},{"step":"The bird advantage can be derived from first principles (smaller cell bodies, no myelin, cheaper energy per neuron) and summarised as a conversion factor of 2.84.","assessment":"unsupported","note":"The derivations only re-arrange assumed cell volumes to land on the densities already quoted, the input densities (2.15×10⁸ for birds, 7.18×10⁷ for primates) contradict the project's data and the paper's own section 4.1, the data give a ratio nearer 4 to 6, and the energy-use citation points to a paper about dopamine wiring, not glucose consumption."},{"step":"Across deep time the cerebellum hogs 70 to 80 percent of mammal neurons, and human ancestors show a 21-fold burst in neuron growth rate.","assessment":"weak","note":"The elephant and human cerebellum numbers check out, but five of the file's cetaceans have 22 to 31 percent cerebellar neurons and the marmoset 57 percent, so the '70 to 80 percent universal' rule fails in the paper's own table, and the 21-fold figure rests on imputed fossil neuron counts and a cherry-picked window."}],"conclusions":[{"conclusion":"A single universal brain-to-body power law is a statistical artifact; lineages evolve along separate grade lines.","assessment":"partly","note":"Separate clade lines really do fit better in the project's data, but the headline statistics quoted for this (slope 0.8641, R² 0.73, AIC gap 260.7, lambda 0.912) are either stale, invalidly computed or hard-coded, so the strength of the evidence is overstated."},{"conclusion":"Cognitive capacity tracks neuron count and packing density, not brain volume, and a factor of 2.84 maps bird brains onto the primate curve.","assessment":"partly","note":"The data do show birds packing roughly 5 to 10 times more neurons per gram of forebrain than primates of similar brain size, which supports the qualitative point, but the specific densities, the raven-versus-pig comparison and the 2.84 factor do not come from this dataset and the paper never measures cognition."},{"conclusion":"Bird neurons run on 2.78 times less energy each, so the avian pallium is a blueprint for efficient neuromorphic hardware.","assessment":"not_supported","note":"No energy data exist in the project files; the figure is derived from assumed geometry that is set up to give the quoted answer, and the empirical confirmation cited is a paper about dopaminergic innervation."},{"conclusion":"Human ancestors experienced a runaway 21-fold acceleration in neuron growth driven by tools, fire, language and culture.","assessment":"partly","note":"The hominin lineage did add neurons far faster than a 340-million-year average, but the fossil neuron counts are imputed from brain size using the very primate scaling rule being tested, the vertebrate baseline uses a living platypus as a stand-in ancestor, and nothing in the data links the rate to tools or culture."}],"inconsistencies":["The text says the pooled regression uses 202 species with slope 0.8641 and R² 0.73; the data file has 277 rows, the auto-generated macros say 277 with slope 0.7955 and R² 0.58, and Figure 1 prints 0.80 and 0.58.","The AIC gap is 260.7 in the text and abstract but 473.0 in the macros file; the 473.0 comes from comparing a fit on 277 rows of one file with fits on 54 taxa of another, which is not a valid comparison.","The grade-shift table is labelled as a phylogenetic (PGLS) analysis with lambda 0.912, yet every slope, intercept and R² in it equals a plain least-squares fit, and the generating script simply assigns lambda = 0.912 as a constant.","The three cellular-scaling intercepts in section 4.1 (7.6481, 7.4215, 8.0123) do not match the fits that produce the quoted slopes (7.8642, 7.4879, 7.9391).","Figure 2 panel D reports slopes of 0.78, 0.55 and 1.62 for primate, other-mammal and bird cellular scaling, while the text reports 0.7727, 0.6471 and 1.1600 for the same relationships.","The raven pallial density is 2.35×10⁸ per gram in the text but 1.22×10⁸ in the data; Figure 2 panel C's label says about 2×10⁸ while its plotted corvid points sit near 1.2–1.6×10⁸.","The pig is 125 g with 1.05×10⁹ neurons in the text and in the Figure 1 annotation, but 180 g with 1.5×10⁹ neurons in the 54-taxon file; the annotation arrow in Figure 1 points to where no data point is.","Primate cortical density is 1.4–5.1×10⁷ per gram in section 4.1, 4.0–9.0×10⁷ in section 4.3.1 and 7.18×10⁷ in section 4.3.4; only the first range agrees with the data.","Section 5 says the elephant's 5.6 billion cortical neurons are fewer than a rhesus macaque's; the data file gives the macaque 1.71 billion.","Section 5 calls 70 to 80 percent cerebellar neurons a universal mammal feature, but the dolphin, orca, humpback and sperm whale rows show 22 to 31 percent and the marmoset 57 percent.","The background rate 0.01538 per million years is said to use 2.7×10⁸ neurons for an early mammal ancestor, but that input gives 0.01527; 0.01538 results only from the file's 2.8×10⁸ platypus value, and no such ancestor appears in the data.","The abstract describes the 'calibrated species' file as biological, but it contains synthetic computers, quantum systems, human-animal mutualisms and individual organs, including a pigeon organ assigned a 120 kg body.","The taxa list in section 2.1 names 53 animals while the file has 54 (the desert ant is omitted), and the 'empirical confirmation' of neuron energy use is cited to a paper whose title is about dopaminergic innervation."],"bottom_line":"A reader can trust the qualitative picture: in this dataset birds pack far more neurons per gram of forebrain than mammals, primates keep density higher than other mammals, and clade-specific lines beat a single universal line. Almost every headline number beyond the three cellular slopes (the raven density, the pig comparison, the pooled fit, the AIC gap, the 2.84 factor, the energy saving and the 21-fold hominin burst) is stale, unreproducible from the project's data, or built on assumed inputs, and should not be quoted until the manuscript is regenerated from the current files.","pdf":"papers/paper2.pdf","corrected_docx":"","pdf_stale":false},{"id":3,"title":"The Speed-Accuracy-Energy Pareto Frontier in Deliberative Biological Nervous Systems and Synthetic Reasoning Models","plain_title":"Thinking longer costs energy, in brains and AI","thesis":"Brains and AI reasoning models both cut their mistakes by thinking longer, and both pay for it in time and energy along one shared trade-off curve.","summary":"The paper argues that fast reflexes (a fly steadying itself in 5 ms) are cheap but make many mistakes, while slow deliberation (a rat pausing at a maze fork, a crow planning tool use, a swarm of bees choosing a nest, an AI model writing thousands of hidden reasoning tokens) cuts mistakes but costs time and energy. It writes down a formula in which accuracy grows with the logarithm of thinking time, multiplies power by time to get the energy of each decision, and compares energy per resolved bit across animals and GPU clusters. It then claims to fit one 'Pareto surface' linking error, latency and power across 950 catalog systems and 25 hardware substrates. Almost every number in the paper turns out to be either typed into the text or hard-coded in the figure script rather than computed from the project's data files.","claims":[{"claim":"One AI reasoning run of 8,192 hidden tokens on 8 H100 GPUs uses a certain amount of electrical energy.","section":"4.2","paper_value":"5.6 kW for 120 s = 6.72×10⁵ J (82 J per token)","data_value":"8 × 700 W (H100 power in the hardware file) = 5.6 kW; × 120 s = 6.72×10⁵ J; ÷ 8,192 = 82.0 J per token","source":"cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv (H100 power 700 W) plus the paper's own 120 s and 8,192 tokens","verdict":"matches","note":"I multiplied 5,600 W by 120 s and divided by 8,192 tokens; the arithmetic is right, but the same run is given as 6.30×10⁵ J (14 kW × 45 s) in section 2.4.3 and 3.0×10⁶ J (25 kW × 120 s) in the section 3 table, so the paper itself does not agree on this number."},{"claim":"An AI reasoning run dissipates vastly more energy than a human thinking about the same kind of problem.","section":"4.2","paper_value":"five orders of magnitude (about 100,000 times), and in the next sentence 56-fold","data_value":"6.72×10⁵ J ÷ 1.2×10⁴ J = 56 times, which is 1.7 orders of magnitude, not five","source":"Arithmetic on the paper's own numbers (20 W × 600 s = 12,000 J for the human; 5.6 kW × 120 s for the AI)","verdict":"not_reproducible","note":"I recomputed both ratios: 56 is correct for the numbers given, but '56-fold' and 'five orders of magnitude' describe the same gap and differ by a factor of about 1,800; the comparison also pits 120 s of AI against 600 s of human thinking, so it is not like-for-like."},{"claim":"The AI cluster spends far more energy per resolved bit of uncertainty than a human brain or a rat hippocampus.","section":"2.4.3","paper_value":"1.26×10⁵ J per bit; nearly 500 times the human brain; 8.4×10⁶ times the rat","data_value":"not in the data (bits resolved are assumed, not measured); arithmetic gives 14,000 W × 45 s = 6.30×10⁵ J ÷ 5 bits = 1.26×10⁵ J/bit; ÷ 266.7 J/bit (human) = 472 times; ÷ 0.015 J/bit (rat) = 8.4×10⁶ times","source":"Arithmetic only; no project file records bits resolved or deliberation power for these systems","verdict":"prose_only","note":"I recomputed all five energy-per-bit values and both ratios and they follow from the stated inputs, but the inputs (5.0 bits, 4.5 bits, 14 kW for 8 GPUs) are assumptions, and the paper's own marginal-cost formula would give a different number than the simple average it actually reports."},{"claim":"The human brain running at 20 W spends a tiny amount of energy per synaptic operation.","section":"4.2","paper_value":"0.133 pJ per synaptic operation","data_value":"20 W ÷ 1.5×10¹⁴ operations per second = 1.33×10⁻¹³ J = 0.133 pJ (neural timeseries); but the hardware file lists a cortical synapse at 1×10⁻¹⁴ J = 0.01 pJ, 13 times lower","source":"neural_sensory_evolution_timeseries.csv (human brain row: 20 W, 1.5×10¹⁴ ops/s); cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv (cortical synapse row)","verdict":"matches","note":"I divided the brain power by the brain's operation rate in the timeseries file and got exactly 0.133 pJ, though the project's two data files disagree with each other by a factor of 13 on this quantity."},{"claim":"A rat deliberating at a maze fork uses a small, measurable amount of energy and climbs from near-chance to high accuracy.","section":"2.4.2 and 2.4.3","paper_value":"0.015 W for 0.85 s = 0.0128 J; accuracy 0.52 to 0.91 with fit coefficient 0.178 and R² = 0.94","data_value":"rat brain row: 0.12 W and 3.5 s deliberation, giving 0.42 J (33 times more); no accuracy or fit data anywhere in the project","source":"neural_sensory_evolution_timeseries.csv (brown rat row); catalog Systems sheet","verdict":"differs","note":"I multiplied the data file's rat brain power by its deliberation time and got 0.42 J versus the paper's 0.0128 J; the paper may mean only the hippocampus, but no file gives that, and the catalog id the paper cites for the rat resolves to a raven-and-wolf foraging partnership, not a rat."},{"claim":"A New Caledonian crow pausing before a multi-step tool puzzle spends a certain energy and jumps from 18% to 84% success.","section":"2.4.2 and 2.4.3","paper_value":"0.18 W for 8.0 s = 1.44 J; fit coefficient 0.241, R² = 0.91","data_value":"crow brain row: 1.8 W and 12 s deliberation, giving 21.6 J (15 times more); no success-rate data in the project","source":"neural_sensory_evolution_timeseries.csv (New Caledonian crow / raven row)","verdict":"differs","note":"I recomputed power × time from the project's crow row and got 21.6 J, ten times the paper's power and 15 times its energy; the 18% and 84% figures appear only in the text, and the catalog id cited for the crow resolves to the extinct small-bodied human Homo floresiensis."},{"claim":"A honeybee swarm choosing a nest site over four hours spends a certain energy and resolves about 3 bits.","section":"2.4.3","paper_value":"0.45 W for 14,400 s = 6,480 J (2.03×10³ J per bit)","data_value":"honeybee swarm row: collective power 20 W; × 14,400 s = 2.88×10⁵ J, 44 times more","source":"collective_organization_evolution.csv (honeybee quorum-sensing swarm row)","verdict":"differs","note":"I multiplied the project's swarm power (20 W) by the paper's four hours and got 2.88×10⁵ J versus 6,480 J; the catalog id the paper cites for the bee swarm resolves to the lamprey's basal ganglia."},{"claim":"One Pareto surface relating error, latency and power fits 25 calibrated biological and digital substrates.","section":"5","paper_value":"latency exponent 0.428 ± 0.035, power exponent 0.312 ± 0.041, R² = 0.884, N = 25, p < 10⁻¹²","data_value":"the hardware file has 33 rows and no error column at all, so the fit cannot be run; fitting the surface to the 10 points hard-coded in the figure script gives exponents 0.09 and 0.03 with R² 0.89; and with N = 25 and R² = 0.884 the smallest possible p is about 5×10⁻¹¹","source":"cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv (33 rows, columns for latency, power and energy per operation but none for error); scripts/generate_paper3_figures.py","verdict":"not_reproducible","note":"I checked the file (33 substrates, no error-rate column), tried the fit on the only error numbers in the project (the ten points typed into the figure script) and got completely different exponents, and ran an F-test showing the stated p-value is impossible for the stated N and R²."},{"claim":"A blowfly's gyroscopic reflex takes about 5 ms at a tiny power and energy.","section":"3 table and 3.1","paper_value":"0.005 s, 0.0001 W, 5.0×10⁻⁷ J per saccade","data_value":"4.67×10⁻³ s, 5×10⁻⁴ W, 2.3×10⁻⁶ J (5 times the power, 4.6 times the energy)","source":"cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv (blowfly haltere gyroscopic feedback row)","verdict":"differs","note":"I read the blowfly row of the hardware file and checked that its power × latency equals its listed energy; the paper's table uses a power and energy about five times lower than the project's own entry."},{"claim":"The catalog behind the paper has a stated size.","section":"Header, Abstract, 2.4, 3, 5","paper_value":"950 systems, 2,616 measurements across 2,624 observation ids, 1,275 sources, 64 sub-epochs; 25 hardware architectures","data_value":"975 systems, 2,641 observation ids, 1,300 sources, 64 sub-epochs; 33 hardware rows","source":"Systema_Intelligentiae_Catalog.xlsx (Systems, Measurements, Sources, Planetary sheets); cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv","verdict":"differs","note":"I counted ids in each sheet; only the 64 sub-epochs match, every other count is 25 lower than the file, and the internal review file quotes a third set of counts (670 systems, 1,055 sources, 2,073 observations)."}],"rationale":[{"step":"Reflexes are fast and cheap but make many mistakes on new situations; deliberation is slow and costly but more accurate.","assessment":"weak","note":"The idea is reasonable, but the paper's own text says the blowfly reflex errs under 2% of the time while its figure plots the same reflex at 45% error, and no error-rate data exists in the project to settle it."},{"step":"Error falls by a fixed mathematical law as thinking steps increase, and energy is power × time, so squeezing out the last errors costs exponentially more energy.","assessment":"weak","note":"The paper uses four different laws for error versus thinking (exponential in section 2.2, logarithmic gain in 2.4.2, power law in 4, stretched exponential in 5), and its claim that steps must grow exponentially contradicts its own exponential-decay equation, under which steps grow only logarithmically."},{"step":"The same logarithmic gain law, with similar coefficients, fits rats, crows, bee swarms and AI reasoning models.","assessment":"unsupported","note":"No accuracy-versus-time data exists in the project for any of the four, the fits and R² values are typed into the text, the catalog ids cited for rat, crow, bee and human point to other organisms, and for the AI case the stated coefficients give an accuracy gain of 1.29, more than 100 percentage points."},{"step":"AI deliberation costs enormously more energy per decision than biological deliberation, because GPUs shuttle weights between memory and compute while neurons store weights where they compute.","assessment":"weak","note":"The direction is plausible and the 150 to 200 pJ per byte of memory traffic matches the hardware file's memory-bound H100 row (200 pJ), but the size of the gap is stated as 56 times, about 500 times and 100,000 times in different places, and the power of the same 8-GPU box is given as 5.6 kW, 14 kW and 25 kW."},{"step":"All of this lies on one universal Pareto surface fitted across the catalog.","assessment":"unsupported","note":"The data file the fit supposedly uses has no error column, the figure draws a hand-chosen curve rather than a fit, and six of the ten plotted systems sit inside the zone the paper calls forbidden."}],"conclusions":[{"conclusion":"Test-time reasoning in AI is the physical rediscovery of biological deliberation, obeying the same law.","assessment":"not_supported","note":"Nothing in the project data tests this; the four 'fits' exist only as sentences, and the analogy between reasoning tokens and hippocampal replay is asserted rather than measured."},{"conclusion":"Digital AI pays a huge energy toll per decision compared with brains, so future hardware must co-locate memory and compute.","assessment":"partly","note":"The hardware file does show a GPU memory fetch at 2×10⁻¹⁰ J against a synapse at 10⁻¹⁴ J (20,000 times), and 8 GPUs for two minutes really is around 10⁵ to 10⁶ J, so the direction holds, but the paper's own magnitudes contradict each other by factors of 4 to 1,800."},{"conclusion":"A universal speed-accuracy-energy Pareto surface with exponents 0.428 and 0.312 governs all 950 systems.","assessment":"not_supported","note":"The fit cannot be reproduced from any project file, its p-value is impossible for the stated sample size, and the figure's points violate the surface."},{"conclusion":"Brains route 99% of inputs through cheap reflexes and reserve deliberation for novel, high-stakes moments, and AI should copy this.","assessment":"not_supported","note":"The 99% figure has no source or data behind it anywhere in the manuscript or the project."}],"inconsistencies":["The energy of one AI reasoning run is 6.72×10⁵ J in section 4.2 (5.6 kW × 120 s), 6.30×10⁵ J in section 2.4.3 (14 kW × 45 s) and 3.0×10⁶ J in the section 3 table (25 kW × 120 s); the same 8-GPU H100 box is given as 5.6 kW in one place and 14 kW in another.","Section 4.2 calls the brain-versus-AI energy gap 'five orders of magnitude' and, two sentences later, '56-fold'; the paper's own numbers give 56 (about 1.7 orders of magnitude).","The header and abstract say 950 systems, 2,616 measurements, 2,624 observation ids and 1,275 sources; the catalog file holds 975 systems, 2,641 observation ids and 1,300 sources, and the internal review file says 670 systems, 1,055 sources and 2,073 observations.","The paper says the hardware file holds 25 calibrated architectures and fits N = 25; the file has 33 rows and no error-rate column, so the section 5 fit could not have been run on it.","The catalog ids the paper attaches to its flagship systems resolve to different things in the catalog: the 'rat hippocampus' id is a raven-and-wolf foraging partnership, the 'crow' id is Homo floresiensis, the 'honeybee swarm' id is the lamprey basal ganglia, the 'human neocortex' id is the sea-slug gill-withdrawal reflex, the 'blowfly' id is the fruit-fly connectome, and the 'mantis shrimp' id is a snapping shrimp (a different animal); the frontier-AI id is a 100,000-GPU cluster drawing 1.2×10⁸ W in the timeseries file, not an 8-GPU box.","Of the 28 footnotes, only one (Bartol et al. 2015) carries a catalog source id that actually points to that paper; the other 27 ids point to unrelated works, and several cited papers (Landauer 1961, Redish 2016, Snell et al. 2024, Hoffmann et al. 2022, Friston 2010, Kahneman 2011) are not in the source registry at all.","The rat's final error is 9% in section 2.4.2 (accuracy 0.91), under 5% in section 3.2 and 5% in Figure 1; the number of sweeps is 4 to 12 in section 2.4.2 and 4 to 8 in the table; section 2.4.2 attributes the sweeps to theta rhythm while section 3.2 and the table attribute them to sharp-wave ripples.","The deliberation gain law applied to the AI case with the stated coefficient (0.214) and time constant (1.8 s) over 750 s gives a gain of 1.29, which is impossible if accuracy is a fraction between 0 and 1 as it is for the rat and crow examples.","The symbol alpha means a gain coefficient of 0.18 to 0.27 in section 2.4.2 and a latency exponent of 0.428 in section 5; the energy-per-bit formula in 2.4.3 is a marginal cost but every number under it is a plain average (energy divided by bits).","Figure 1 draws the 'Pareto boundary' from a hand-typed formula (0.5 × exp(−1.5 × t^0.4) + 0.005), not from a fit, its exponent 0.4 is not the paper's 0.428, it has no power term, and six of the ten plotted systems (mantis shrimp, Mauthner escape, human saccade, rat, AlphaZero and more) lie below it, inside the zone the text calls forbidden.","Figure 2 is a hard-coded power law (exponent 0.52) that gives 4.8% error at 8,192 tokens and 9.9% at 2,048 tokens, while Figure 1 plots the same two systems at 0.8% and 2% error.","Figure 3 bar values disagree with the hardware file: SpiNNaker 2 at 8.3×10⁹ versus 6.67×10¹¹ operations per joule, NorthPole 2.0×10¹² versus 2.5×10¹², B200 1.2×10¹³ versus 4.5×10¹².","Section 3.1 says the blowfly reflex errs under 2% of the time on predictable gusts, yet Figure 1 plots it at 45% error; the table's blowfly power (0.0001 W) is five times lower than the project's own blowfly row (0.0005 W).","The manuscript never refers to any of its three figures in the text."],"bottom_line":"A careful reader can rely on the simple arithmetic (power × time gives the stated joules, 5.6 kW for 120 s is 6.72×10⁵ J, and GPUs really do spend thousands of times more energy per operation than synapses in the project's hardware file). Nothing else is checkable: the animal and AI deliberation fits, the universal Pareto surface, the energy-per-bit ratios and the figures are typed-in numbers that contradict each other and the project's data, and the catalog ids point to the wrong organisms.","pdf":"papers/paper3.pdf","corrected_docx":"","pdf_stale":false},{"id":4,"title":"The Exosomatic Ratchet: Decoupling Informational Persistence from Somatic Biomass from Acheulean Handaxes to the Modern Datasphere","plain_title":"How humans moved memory out of the brain","thesis":"Human intelligence became cumulative and planetary because memory moved out of living brains into durable objects, from stone tools to clay, paper and data centres.","summary":"The paper argues that brains are capped by lifespan, skull size and a four-item working memory, so humans had to store knowledge outside the body. It proposes a ratchet number that says when a culture keeps more knowledge than it loses, and a persistence capacity (bits stored times years they last) that it evaluates for stone tools, oral tradition, clay tablets, printed books and cloud storage. It also tabulates brain size and neuron counts for 14 fossil hominins, sketches ten ages of planetary computing, and claims that storage density rose 21 orders of magnitude while retention time fell, and that digital computing now exceeds all human brains combined.","claims":[{"claim":"Storage density of human-made memory rose 21 orders of magnitude from the Acheulean handaxe to DNA storage.","section":"Abstract; Section 5 table; Figure 1","paper_value":"21 orders of magnitude (10⁻⁴ bits per cm³ for a handaxe up to 10¹⁷ bits per cm³ for DNA storage)","data_value":"No handaxe density exists in the project data. Using the paper's own 100 to 1,000 bits per handaxe and a hand-sized stone of about 300 cm³ gives 0.3 to 3 bits per cm³, so the span is about 14 orders to today's flash memory and 16 to 17 orders to the paper's DNA figure; the memory ledger's own range from clay tablets (0.23 bits per cm³) to flash drives (1.8×10¹⁴) is 15 orders.","source":"Recomputed from the manuscript's stated bits per tool; compared with volumetric densities in paper0_memory_persistence_ledger.csv","verdict":"not_reproducible","note":"The stated endpoints do divide to 21 orders, but the low endpoint is impossible: 100 to 1,000 bits at 10⁻⁴ bits per cm³ means a handaxe of 1 to 10 cubic metres of stone, and the figure's five deployed technologies only span 10⁻⁴ to 10¹⁴, 18 orders even accepting the handaxe value."},{"claim":"The engraved Blombos ochre stores a few thousand bits of abstract pattern at about a tenth of a bit per cubic centimetre.","section":"Abstract; Section 5, Epoch II","paper_value":"about 4,200 bits; 10⁻¹ bits per cm³","data_value":"4,200 bits in the catalog measurement log (matches); no volume or density is recorded, and 4,200 bits at 0.1 bits per cm³ implies a 42-litre block, while the real engraved piece is a few centimetres long (tens of cm³, so roughly 50 to 100 bits per cm³)","source":"Systema_Intelligentiae_Catalog.xlsx Measurements sheet (Blombos ochre capacity row); density recomputed as bits divided by implied volume","verdict":"not_reproducible","note":"The bit count matches the catalog exactly, but dividing it by any realistic volume gives a density about 500 to 1,000 times higher than the manuscript's 0.1 bits per cm³, which pulls the low end of Figure 1 up by about three orders."},{"claim":"Clay tablets store about a hundred bits per cubic centimetre and printed paper about a hundred thousand.","section":"Abstract; Section 5 table","paper_value":"10² bits per cm³ (fired clay); 10⁵ bits per cm³ (rag paper)","data_value":"0.23 bits per cm³ for inscribed clay and stone; 8,000 bits per cm³ for rag paper (12,000 for wood-pulp paper)","source":"paper0_memory_persistence_ledger.csv, volumetric_density_bits_per_cm3 column; book density also recomputed from the paper's own 5×10⁶ bits per book","verdict":"differs","note":"The ledger gives clay about 400 times lower and paper about 12 times lower than the manuscript; the paper's own 5 million bits per book at 10⁵ bits per cm³ would need a 50 cm³ book, whereas a real 500 to 1,000 cm³ book gives 5,000 to 10,000 bits per cm³, in line with the ledger."},{"claim":"The world's unique active cloud and archival storage in 2026 is about 15 zettabytes.","section":"Abstract; Section 2.5.3, item 5; Section 4, Age X","paper_value":"1.2×10²³ bits (about 15 zettabytes), within a 150 zettabyte gross datasphere","data_value":"1.2×10²³ bits installed exosomatic storage, 2026 (tape 1.8×10²² + hard disks 5.4×10²² + flash 4.8×10²² = 1.2×10²³); the planetary time series gives the same 1.2×10²³ for the present","source":"paper0_memory_persistence_ledger.csv (installed_capacity_2026_bits) and planetary_intelligence_timeseries.csv (m_exo_installed_bits, final row)","verdict":"matches","note":"I summed the three digital media rows of the ledger and converted 15 zettabytes to bits (15×10²¹ × 8 = 1.2×10²³); both agree with the manuscript."},{"claim":"Modern storage must be refreshed every 15 years, giving a persistence capacity of 1.8×10²⁴ bit-years for the datasphere.","section":"Abstract; Section 2.5.3, item 5; Section 5 table","paper_value":"retention 15 years; persistence 1.8×10²⁴ bit-years","data_value":"ledger: weighted mean retention 8.3 years and persistence 9.96×10²³ bit-years (sum of tape 4.5×10²³, disk 3.78×10²³, flash 1.68×10²³); planetary time series: 3.5 years and 4.2×10²³ bit-years","source":"paper0_memory_persistence_ledger.csv (mean_retention_lifetime_yr, persistence_integral_bit_yr); planetary_intelligence_timeseries.csv (tau_exo_unpowered_years, phi_exo_unpowered_bit_years)","verdict":"differs","note":"The manuscript's multiplication (1.2×10²³ × 15 = 1.8×10²⁴) is correct, but 15 years is the top of its own quoted 5-to-15-year range, while the project's ledger gives 8.3 years and 9.96×10²³ bit-years, 1.8 times smaller, and the time series 3.5 years and 4.2×10²³."},{"claim":"One human brain holds about 88 terabytes in its synapses, performs about 150 trillion synaptic operations a second, and runs on 20 watts.","section":"Sections 2.3, 2.4, 4 (Age VIII) and 7","paper_value":"1.5×10¹⁴ synapses × 4.7 bits = 7.05×10¹⁴ bits ≈ 88.1 TB; 150 TOPS; 20 W (0.133 pJ per operation)","data_value":"arithmetic reproduces: log₂(26) = 4.70 bits, 7.05×10¹⁴ bits ÷ 8 = 88.1 TB, 20 W ÷ 1.5×10¹⁴ per s = 0.133 pJ; the ledger uses the same 4.7 bits per synapse (3×10²⁷ synapses worldwide × 4.7 = 1.41×10²⁸ bits); the hominin table's RAM, TB and TOPS columns all follow from neurons × 1,743 synapses per neuron","source":"Recomputed by hand; cross-checked against paper0_memory_persistence_ledger.csv synaptic row and planetary_intelligence_timeseries.csv n_synapses_global","verdict":"matches","note":"Every number in this chain follows from the two literature inputs (1.5×10¹⁴ synapses, 26 synapse states) and a 1 Hz firing rate, and the ledger adopts the same per-synapse figure; the only wrinkle is that the paper also quotes 11.2 W per kg, which would give 15.7 W for a 1.4 kg brain, not 20 W."},{"claim":"Fourteen fossil hominins trace a tripling of brain size with specific ages, body masses and neuron counts.","section":"Section 3 table; Figure 2","paper_value":"e.g. Homo erectus 1.0 million years ago, 60 kg, 950 cm³, 62 billion neurons; Australopithecus afarensis 3.2 million years ago; Homo habilis 1.9; Homo heidelbergensis 0.43; Neanderthal 0.06","data_value":"brain volumes and neuron counts match for all 11 taxa present in the file; ages differ for six (afarensis 3.5, boisei 2.0, habilis 2.3, erectus 1.8, heidelbergensis 0.6, Neanderthal 0.1 million years ago) and body mass for two (erectus 55 kg, heidelbergensis 70 kg); Sahelanthropus, Homo antecessor and the Jebel Irhoud skull are not in the file","source":"cns_structural_evolution.csv, columns divergence_mya, body_mass_g, total_brain_mass_g, total_neurons, compared row by row","verdict":"differs","note":"Neuron counts and volumes come straight from the file, but 3 of the 14 taxa are not in it and the dates of 6 others were changed (Homo erectus by 0.8 million years), so the table is only partly from the cited data."},{"claim":"In 2026 digital machines out-compute all human brains combined: 3.5×10²⁴ operations a second on 450 GW versus 1.215×10²⁴ synaptic operations a second on 162 GW.","section":"Section 4, Age X; Section 5, Epoch V; Figure 3","paper_value":"digital 3.5×10²⁴ FLOP/s at 450 GW; human 1.215×10²⁴ synaptic operations per second at 162 GW; total 4.7×10²⁴","data_value":"device ledger: 1.89×10²² executed operations a second (peak 7.6×10²²) at 80 GW; time series: digital 1.89×10²², human-plus-animal synaptic 3×10²⁶, total planetary 2.8×10³³ (dominated by microbial biochemistry)","source":"paper0_device_inventory_ledger.csv total row; planetary_intelligence_timeseries.csv final row (c_digital_ops_sec, c_neural_ops_sec, c_total_ops_sec, p_digital_it_watts)","verdict":"differs","note":"The human side reproduces (8.1×10⁹ × 1.5×10¹⁴ = 1.215×10²⁴; 8.1×10⁹ × 20 W = 162 GW), but the digital side is 185 times the ledger's executed figure and 46 times its peak, and uses 5.6 times its power; by the project's own data digital computing is 1.6 percent of human synaptic activity, so there is no inversion."},{"claim":"Planetary computing in the first two ages of life ran at about 10¹⁴ operations a second (RNA world) and 10²¹ (prokaryotes).","section":"Section 4, Ten Ages table and Ages I to III","paper_value":"10¹³ to 10¹⁵ operations per second from 6×10³⁶ ribozymes; 10²¹ from 1.2 to 3.1×10³⁰ prokaryotic cells at 1,000 steps a second each","data_value":"the paper's own inputs give 6×10³⁶ to 6×10³⁷ (RNA) and 1.2 to 3.1×10³³ (prokaryotes); the time series gives 2.6×10²¹ for the first protocells and 10³² to 10³³ for the prokaryotic ages","source":"Recomputed from the manuscript's stated molecule and cell counts and rates; compared with planetary_intelligence_timeseries.csv c_total_ops_sec","verdict":"not_reproducible","note":"Multiplying the paper's 6×10³⁶ molecules by its 1 to 10 reactions a second gives 22 orders of magnitude more than it states, and its prokaryote figure is 12 orders below its own cells-times-rate product and 11 to 12 orders below the project time series, so the Ten Ages table and Figure 3 are not derived from the stated inputs."},{"claim":"Small hunter-gatherer bands fall below the cultural ratchet threshold, so skills are lost faster than they are invented.","section":"Section 2.5.2","paper_value":"ratchet number = N × τ × F ÷ μ with N 10 to 100, τ 30 years, F 0.15, μ 500 per year, giving a value below 1","data_value":"not in the data; recomputing gives 0.09 (N = 10) to 0.9 (N = 100), and the value passes 1 at N ≈ 111","source":"Arithmetic on the manuscript's own parameters; formula attributed to Henrich 2004","verdict":"prose_only","note":"The numbers do fall below 1 as stated, but the quantity is called dimensionless while its units work out to years squared, none of the four parameters is measured anywhere in the project, and the result flips above 1 for a band only slightly larger than the paper's own range."}],"rationale":[{"step":"Brains are capped by lifespan, by skull size and heat, and by a four-item working memory, so growing knowledge had to move outside the body.","assessment":"weak","note":"The three limits are real and well cited, but the paper's own table shows brain volume still rising to 1,600 cm³ at 60,000 years ago while the first handaxes date to 1.76 million years ago, so externalisation began long before any ceiling and the ceiling cannot have forced it."},{"step":"A culture accumulates knowledge only when a ratchet number (population × retention × fidelity ÷ loss rate) exceeds one, and external media push it over by lengthening retention and raising copying fidelity.","assessment":"weak","note":"The formula is asserted rather than derived, has units of years squared despite being called dimensionless, and is never evaluated against any project measurement; the only worked example uses chosen parameters."},{"step":"Across five epochs the density of external memory rose about 21 orders of magnitude while unpowered retention time fell from 100,000 years to 15 years.","assessment":"unsupported","note":"The direction holds (the ledger goes from 0.23 bits per cm³ and 5,000 years for clay to 1.8×10¹⁴ bits per cm³ and 3.5 years for flash), but the 21-orders figure rests on a handaxe density that implies a cubic-metre stone and on no measured volume for any artefact."},{"step":"Persistence capacity (bits × years) nonetheless climbed from about 10¹³ bit-years for stone tools to about 10²⁴ for the datasphere, which now survives only through continuous electrical refresh.","assessment":"holds","note":"The ledger gives 5×10¹³ bit-years for clay and stone, 9.96×10²³ for digital media and 20 GW of refresh power, so the shape of the argument is supported even though the paper's 1.8×10²⁴ overstates the ledger by 1.8 times."},{"step":"Digital machines now out-compute all human brains, so intelligence has become non-biological and planetary.","assessment":"unsupported","note":"The project's device ledger puts executed digital operations at 1.89×10²² a second, 1.6 percent of the 1.215×10²⁴ synaptic operations of 8.1 billion human brains and 0.006 percent of all animal synaptic activity, the reverse of the inversion the paper describes."}],"conclusions":[{"conclusion":"Human culture accumulates because memory was moved out of brains into durable external objects, and this is what made intelligence cumulative and planetary.","assessment":"partly","note":"The project time series does show external memory climbing from about 10⁵ bits in the handaxe era to 1.2×10²³ bits today, but the threshold mechanism is never tested and the claim that brain limits forced the move contradicts the paper's own timeline."},{"conclusion":"Storage density of human-made memory has increased by 21 orders of magnitude.","assessment":"not_supported","note":"Bits divided by any realistic artefact volume gives about 14 orders to today's flash memory and 16 to 17 to DNA storage; the ledger's clay-to-flash range is 15 orders."},{"conclusion":"Modern civilisation's memory is far larger but far shorter-lived than ancient media, and survives only by continuous power (the persistence paradox).","assessment":"supported","note":"The ledger gives 1.2×10²³ bits with 8.3-year mean retention and 20 GW refresh power against 10¹⁰ bits with 5,000-year retention and zero power for clay, so the paradox holds even though the paper's 15 years and 1.8×10²⁴ bit-years overstate it."},{"conclusion":"Digital computing has overtaken the combined computing of all human brains.","assessment":"not_supported","note":"The paper's 3.5×10²⁴ operations a second is 185 times the executed total and 46 times the peak total in the project's own device ledger, which leaves digital computing well below human synaptic activity."}],"inconsistencies":["The handaxe is said to hold 100 to 1,000 bits at 10⁻⁴ bits per cm³, which requires a stone of 1 to 10 cubic metres; the Blombos ochre is said to hold 4,200 bits at 0.1 bits per cm³, which requires a 42-litre block. The bit counts and the densities cannot both be right.","The abstract gives DNA storage as 10¹⁷ bits per cm³ while Figure 1 plots 2.15×10¹⁷ and the memory ledger gives 2.4×10²¹ bits per cm³ for DNA; the figure's number equals a published figure of 215 petabytes per gram, a bytes-per-gram quantity, which suggests a unit mix-up.","Retention for cloud storage is 15 years in every table and formula, but the same paragraph calls it a 5-to-15-year horizon, the memory ledger gives 8.3 years and the planetary time series 3.5 years.","The Ten Ages table and its text disagree: hominin Paleolithic compute is 1.5×10²⁰ to 10²² in the table but 1.5×10¹⁹ to 7.5×10²⁰ in the text; RNA-world hereditary code is 200 to 500 bits in the table but 200 to 1,000 bits in the text, and the text's 100-letter error threshold contradicts its own 100-to-500-letter ribozymes.","Figure 3 plots 1.2×10²⁴ and 1.3×10²⁴ operations a second for the hominin and historical ages, while the Ten Ages table gives at most 10²² and 3.5×10²³ for the same ages.","The Anthropocene total of 4.7×10²⁴ operations a second counts only humans and machines, yet the paper's own Age VII puts non-human animal brains at 10²⁴ to 3×10²⁶, and the project time series gives 3×10²⁶ for all animal synaptic activity today.","The paper says it tracks 14 hominin taxa; the table has 15 rows (including the chimpanzee), the data file has 11 of them, and Figure 2 plots only 12 (dropping Australopithecus sediba and Homo floresiensis).","Six hominin dates and two body masses differ from the project's CNS file, most sharply Homo erectus at 1.0 versus 1.8 million years ago.","The paper's epoch labels are shifted by about two steps from the project's own epoch table: in the project table the handaxe era, Blombos ochre, Uruk cuneiform and Gutenberg fall two sub-epochs earlier than the labels the manuscript gives them, so cross-references to the planetary time series point at the wrong rows.","Seven of the catalog entries the manuscript cites describe different objects: the entry given for the Acheulean handaxe is the spear-thrower, the one for Uruk clay tablets is the Antikythera mechanism, the one for the Gutenberg press is Babbage's Difference Engine, the one for the Antikythera mechanism is the Inca quipu, the one for the Analytical Engine is Kelvin's tide predictor, the one for cave murals is the meerkat alarm call, and the one for the modern human is the sea slug Aplysia.","Brain power is given as 20 W for a 1.4 kg brain (14.3 W per kg) but also as 11.2 W per kg, which would be 15.7 W; a 3 kg brain is said to need 45 W, whereas 20 W scaled to 3 kg is 43 W and 11.2 W per kg gives 34 W.","Eukaryotic genomes of 10⁷ to 10⁹ letters are given as 20 to 200 megabytes; at 2 bits per letter they are 2.5 to 250 megabytes.","Oral tradition is dated 30,000 to 10,000 years ago in Section 2 and 36,000 to 10,000 years ago in Section 5; the ledger treats living synaptic memory as lasting weeks (0.055 years), not the 50 years the paper assigns to it.","Printed-book memory for 1450 to 1700 is put at 10¹⁴ bits, while the project time series gives 2×10¹² to 10¹³ bits for the same period, 10 to 50 times less.","The ratchet number is called dimensionless, but population × years × fidelity ÷ (per year) has units of years squared."],"bottom_line":"A reader can rely on the brain-scale arithmetic (88 TB, 150 trillion operations a second, 20 W), on today's 1.2×10²³ bits of storage, and on the broad picture that external memory grew enormously while its unpowered lifetime shrank to a few years. The headline numbers should not be repeated: the 21 orders of magnitude in density rests on impossible artefact volumes, the 15-year retention and 1.8×10²⁴ bit-years overstate the project ledger, the Ten Ages compute figures do not follow from their own inputs, and the project's device ledger shows digital computing at about 1.6 percent of human synaptic activity, not ahead of it.","pdf":"papers/paper4.pdf","corrected_docx":"","pdf_stale":false},{"id":5,"title":"The Evolution of Collective Organization and Multi-Agent Superorganisms: From Eusocial Insect Castes, Termite Cathedral Mounds, and Scale-Free Vertebrate Flocks to the 8.1-Billion-Human Planetary Superorganism","plain_title":"How living things team up into giant group minds","thesis":"Groups of organisms, from bacterial films and ant colonies to bird flocks and human civilization, beat the limits of a single brain by coordinating many brains, and humans did it by stacking layers of organization on top of ever-larger outside memory such as writing and computers.","summary":"The paper lines up 22 kinds of collective, from 3.5-billion-year-old microbial mats to today's 8.1 billion networked humans, and gives each a group size, a total neuron count, a memory and a computing rate. It argues that insects built superorganisms through sterile worker castes and chemical trails, that flocks and schools coordinate by each animal tracking about seven neighbours, and that humans scaled up through layered hierarchies plus outside record-keeping. The key quantitative claim is that every tenfold growth in a human society needed roughly a 280-fold growth in stored outside information. We recomputed the main numbers from the project's own data files and the figure-building script.","claims":[{"claim":"Across the eight human stages, stored outside memory grows as a steep power of population, with a very tight fit.","section":"Section 2.4, point 3, and Figure 3B","paper_value":"log10(outside memory) = 2.45 × log10(population) − 2.80, R² = 0.984; about 280 times more storage per tenfold population","data_value":"Refit of the script's own eight points: slope 2.10, intercept −1.38, R² = 0.90; that slope means about 127 times more storage per tenfold population. The stated line explains only 87 percent of the spread of those points (R² = 0.87).","source":"The eight outside-memory values are typed by hand in scripts/build_paper5_collective_organization.py (bands 35 and 150 people, 3,000, 20,000, 80,000, 60 million, 300 million, 8.1 billion); collective_organization_evolution.csv has no outside-memory column at all, only a total memory column.","verdict":"not_reproducible","note":"I fitted a straight line to the eight log-log points the figure plots and got slope 2.10 and R² 0.90, not 2.45 and 0.984; the dashed line in the figure is labelled a 'theoretical threshold' in the script, not a regression, and 10 to the power 2.45 is indeed 282, so the '280-fold' follows from the stated slope but not from the data."},{"claim":"A single leafcutter ant colony holds more neurons than a cat brain and about one forty-third of a human brain.","section":"Abstract, Table 1 (Section 3.2)","paper_value":"2.0×10¹² neurons, 2.7 times a domestic cat brain, about 1/43 of a human brain","data_value":"8.0×10⁶ ants × 2.5×10⁵ neurons = 2.0×10¹² neurons (matches the CSV), but the project's species file gives a cat 1.2×10⁹ neurons and a human 8.6×10¹⁰, so the colony is about 1,700 cat brains and about 23 human brains, not 1/43 of one.","source":"collective_organization_evolution.csv row for leafcutter ants (group size × neurons per individual); cleaned_biological_species.csv rows for domestic cat, human and leafcutter worker.","verdict":"differs","note":"The product is right but both brain comparisons are off by about a thousandfold in opposite directions; the species file also lists 4.0×10⁵ neurons per leafcutter worker, which would make the colony 3.2×10¹² neurons."},{"claim":"A termite mound colony has about as many neurons as a macaque monkey brain, and Table 1 gives each insect colony's computing rate.","section":"Table 1 (Section 3.2)","paper_value":"5.00×10¹¹ neurons (about 0.7 of a macaque brain); 5.00×10¹³ operations per second for termites, 4.80×10¹² for the bee hive, 2.00×10¹⁴ for leafcutters","data_value":"2.0×10⁶ × 2.5×10⁵ = 5.0×10¹¹ neurons (matches), but a macaque has 6.37×10⁹ neurons in the species file, so the colony is 78 macaque brains, not 0.7. The CSV computing rates are 5.0×10¹⁴, 4.8×10¹³ and 2.0×10¹⁵, each exactly ten times the table.","source":"collective_organization_evolution.csv (neurons per individual, group size, collective compute); cleaned_biological_species.csv (macaque).","verdict":"differs","note":"I multiplied group size by neurons per individual and compared to the macaque row; I also divided the CSV compute by colony neurons and found the CSV assumes 1,000 operations per neuron per second while Table 1 uses 100, so all four insect rows in Table 1 are ten times below the data file."},{"claim":"A starling murmuration of 100,000 birds has a huge collective neuron count.","section":"Section 3.3, point 2, and Figure 2A","paper_value":"1.9×10¹⁴ neurons","data_value":"1.0×10⁵ birds × 1.9×10⁸ neurons per bird = 1.9×10¹³ neurons in the CSV; the species file lists a murmuration at 1.25×10¹³.","source":"collective_organization_evolution.csv starling row; cleaned_biological_species.csv 'Starling murmuration' row; the figure script hard-codes 1.9×10¹⁴.","verdict":"differs","note":"The text and Figure 2 are ten times higher than the data file, which would need 1.9×10⁹ neurons per starling, about four times a real starling's roughly 5×10⁸."},{"claim":"Today's planetary collective combines 8.1 billion human brains with a measured amount of installed storage and silicon computing.","section":"Abstract, Section 3.4 point 5, Section 4","paper_value":"6.97×10²⁰ human neurons; 15 zettabytes = 1.20×10²³ bits of storage; 1.89×10²² silicon operations per second","data_value":"8.1×10⁹ × 8.6×10¹⁰ = 6.97×10²⁰ neurons; 15×10²¹ bytes × 8 = 1.2×10²³ bits; the planetary time series row for 2026 lists 1.2×10²³ installed bits and 1.89×10²² digital operations per second.","source":"collective_organization_evolution.csv (2026 row) and planetary_intelligence_timeseries.csv (2026 row).","verdict":"matches","note":"All three components recompute exactly from the project data; only the way they are added up (next claim) goes wrong."},{"claim":"The 2026 planetary collective's total computing rate, brains plus machines, is given in Table 2.","section":"Table 2 (Section 3.4)","paper_value":"1.02×10²⁴ operations per second (10²⁴ biological + 1.89×10²² silicon); the abstract also gives 1.23×10²⁴ synaptic events per second","data_value":"The CSV lists 7.16×10²² operations per second for the same row, 14 times less; CSV compute for every human stage is 100 operations per neuron per second, Table 2 uses about 1,160.","source":"collective_organization_evolution.csv, collective_compute_ops_sec column for the eight human rows, divided by collective neurons.","verdict":"differs","note":"I divided each Table 2 compute value by its neuron count and did the same for the CSV; all eight human rows in Table 2 are 11 to 14 times above the data file, and the biological figure itself is quoted three ways (1.23×10²⁴, about 1.0×10²⁴, and 10²⁴)."},{"claim":"Human societies grew to 8.1 billion by adding levels of command, each level coordinating a handful of units below it.","section":"Section 2.4, point 2, and Figure 3A","paper_value":"population = base group × branching^(levels − 1), with base 35 to 150, branching 4 to 7, levels rising from 1 to 8","data_value":"The largest value the formula allows is 150 × 7⁷ = 1.2×10⁸ at 8 levels, 65 times short of 8.1 billion; reaching 8.1 billion with 7 to 8 levels needs a branching of 13 to 19, and reaching Rome's 60 million with 5 to 6 levels needs about 18.","source":"Arithmetic on the formula and the stage list in the manuscript; the hierarchy levels in Figure 3A are hand-typed in the build script.","verdict":"not_reproducible","note":"I evaluated the formula for every combination of base and branching the paper allows and none comes within a factor of 20 of the empire or planetary stages, so the level counts in Table 2 and Figure 3A do not follow from the stated law."},{"claim":"Dunbar's brain-size relation caps cohesive primate groups at about 80 to 150 individuals.","section":"Abstract point 3, Section 2.4","paper_value":"log10(group size) = 0.034 + 0.38 × log10(neocortex ratio), giving 80 to 150 for a ratio of 3.2 to 4.1","data_value":"As written the formula gives a group of 1.7 to 1.8 individuals; Dunbar's published 1992 coefficients (0.093 and 3.389) give 148 for a ratio of 4.1.","source":"Arithmetic on the equation in the manuscript; not in the project data.","verdict":"not_reproducible","note":"I plugged the paper's own neocortex ratios into its own equation and got about two individuals, so the coefficients are mistyped even though the 150-person conclusion is the standard literature value."},{"claim":"Spoken relays lose most of their accuracy after a few hops, and a 150-person group has thousands of pairwise relationships.","section":"Section 2.4, points 1 and 3","paper_value":"(0.80)⁶ = 0.262, so 73.8 percent corruption at 4 levels; 150 people form 11,175 pairs; 150 to 8.1 billion is a 5.4×10⁷-fold expansion","data_value":"not in the data; arithmetic recomputed: 0.8⁶ = 0.2621, 150 × 149 / 2 = 11,175, 8.1×10⁹ / 150 = 5.4×10⁷, and the allowed error per hop 0.11/(levels − 1) matches −ln(0.8)/2 = 0.1116.","source":"Formulas in the manuscript; the 20 percent error per hop is an assumed value with no cited measurement.","verdict":"prose_only","note":"Every piece of arithmetic here is correct, but the 20 percent per-hop loss and the 80 percent survival threshold are assumptions, so the 'theorem' is an illustration rather than a measured result."},{"claim":"Each starling tracks a fixed number of about seven nearest neighbours, and this is the best trade-off between flock cohesion and attention.","section":"Section 2.3 and Figure 2B","paper_value":"6.5 ± 0.7 neighbours; optimum at about 7","data_value":"not in the data; the Figure 2B curves are 1 − e^(−k/2.2)(1 + 0.4/k) minus 0.28 × k/15, typed into the script, which peaks at k = 7.2; changing the cost weight from 0.2 to 0.4 moves the peak from 7.9 to 6.4.","source":"Literature value from the cited starling field studies; curve from scripts/build_paper5_collective_organization.py.","verdict":"prose_only","note":"I evaluated the script's formula over k = 1 to 15 and confirmed the peak sits at 7 only because the two constants were chosen to put it there, so Figure 2B illustrates the literature value rather than deriving it."}],"rationale":[{"step":"Single brains hit hard limits on energy, wiring and birth size, so the only way to keep growing intelligence is to link many brains together.","assessment":"weak","note":"The limits are real and cited, but the paper's own numbers clash (5×10⁻¹⁰ watts per neuron versus 20 W / 8.6×10¹⁰ = 2.3×10⁻¹⁰), and nothing in the data tests whether collectives actually reach the computing power a bigger brain would have."},{"step":"Insects became superorganisms through sterile castes and chemical trails, so a colony's neurons add up to a monkey- or cat-sized brain.","assessment":"weak","note":"The neuron totals multiply out correctly, but every brain comparison in Table 1 is off by about a thousandfold (a leafcutter colony is 23 human brains by the paper's own inputs, not 1/43), and adding up neurons says nothing about whether they work like one brain."},{"step":"Flocks and schools coordinate because each animal follows about seven nearest neighbours, the sweet spot between staying together and paying attention.","assessment":"weak","note":"The seven-neighbour figure is a genuine literature result, but the 'derivation' in Figure 2B is a hand-tuned curve, and the collective neuron counts for starlings and herring in the text are ten times the data file."},{"step":"Humans scaled from bands to billions by stacking command levels, and each new level needed far more outside memory to stay honest.","assessment":"unsupported","note":"The level formula cannot reach the empire or planetary populations with the branching the paper allows, the memory fit refits to slope 2.1 with R² 0.90 instead of 2.45 with 0.984, and the Dunbar equation as written gives a group of two."},{"step":"Therefore evolution has found three stable kinds of collective: chemical superorganisms, egalitarian swarms, and the human hierarchy-plus-records society.","assessment":"weak","note":"This is a reasonable way to sort the 22 rows, but it is a classification the authors imposed, not a pattern the data were shown to produce, and the CSV's own human rows show memory per person falling, not rising, from 4.3×10¹³ to 1.5×10¹³ bits."}],"conclusions":[{"conclusion":"Insect colonies evolved superorganism status twice, by different genetic routes, and their combined neuron counts rival small mammal brains.","assessment":"partly","note":"The two routes are well cited and the neuron totals follow from the data, but the mammal-brain comparisons are wrong by about a thousandfold and the colony computing rates are ten times below the project's own file."},{"conclusion":"Egalitarian vertebrate groups coordinate through fixed nearest-neighbour rules rather than fixed distances.","assessment":"partly","note":"This rests on cited field studies, not on anything in the project data, and the figure meant to derive the optimum is a curve tuned to give seven."},{"conclusion":"Every tenfold growth in human society required about a 280-fold growth in outside stored information.","assessment":"not_supported","note":"The eight points behind the claim are typed by hand, refit to a slope of 2.1 (about 127-fold) with R² 0.90, and the project's data file contains no outside-memory column to check them against."},{"conclusion":"Today 8.1 billion humans plus silicon form a single planetary superorganism with a measurable neuron count, storage and compute.","assessment":"partly","note":"The neuron count, 15 zettabytes and silicon rate match the project data exactly, but the combined compute in Table 2 is 14 times the data file and the biological rate is quoted three different ways."}],"inconsistencies":["The abstract gives 1.23×10²⁴ synaptic events per second for humanity; Section 3.4 says about 1.0×10²⁴ and Table 2 says 10²⁴.","Table 2 lists the 2026 collective compute as 1.02×10²⁴ operations per second; the data file gives 7.16×10²² for the same row, and every other human row in Table 2 is 11 to 14 times above the file.","Table 2 memory for 1975 is 3.00×10²³ bits against 1.72×10²² in the data file, and 8.00×10²³ for 2026 against 1.20×10²³.","Table 1 computing rates for termites, ants, bees and leafcutters (5.00×10¹³, 5.00×10¹¹, 4.80×10¹², 2.00×10¹⁴) are each exactly one tenth of the data file's values.","Table 1 says the leafcutter colony's 2.0×10¹² neurons are 2.7 cat brains and 1/43 of a human brain; with the project's own species counts they are about 1,700 cat brains and 23 human brains. The termite 'about 0.7 macaque brain' is 78 macaque brains, the bee hive 'about a marmoset brain' is about 75 marmoset brains, and the supercolony '0.87 of a human brain' is about 870 human brains.","Table 1 gives the army ant colony up to 2.45×10¹¹ neurons, but 7.0×10⁵ ants × 2.5×10⁵ neurons is 1.75×10¹¹.","The text and Figure 2 give starlings 1.9×10¹⁴ collective neurons and herring 10¹⁵; the data file gives 1.9×10¹³ and 1.0×10¹⁴. Wildebeest herds are 4.5×10¹⁵ in the text and 3.75×10¹⁵ in the file.","Group sizes in the text differ from the file: biofilms 10¹⁰ cells versus 10⁹, coral and bryozoan colonies 10⁵ versus 10⁶ polyps.","Dates in the text differ from the file: herring schools about 120 million years ago versus 400; starling flocks about 60 versus 120; honeybees about 85 versus 100; wildebeest about 20 versus 25; elephants about 10 versus 8; siphonophores 520 versus 540.","Section 2.4 says the memory regression runs across the eight human stages, but Figure 3B plots eight hand-typed points that split the forager stage into a 35-person band and a 150-person clan and leave out the 1975 stage of 4 billion people, even though the caption names that stage at about 10¹⁹ bits.","Figure 1B's caption says human collective memory grows faster than population, but in the data file total memory for the human rows grows with population to the power 0.92, and memory per person falls from 4.3×10¹³ bits to 1.5×10¹³ bits between the forager and 2026 rows.","Section 1 states a brain cost of 5×10⁻¹⁰ watts per neuron, but 20 watts divided by the paper's 8.6×10¹⁰ neurons is 2.3×10⁻¹⁰ watts per neuron.","Section 2.2 says a termite colony releases 20 to 50 watts of heat while breathing 1,000 litres of oxygen a day; burning that oxygen releases about 230 watts.","The abstract says the 22 transitions span 3.8 billion years, the oldest row in the file is 3.48 billion years old, and the introduction speaks of a 4.1-billion-year history.","Table 2 gives the forager band range as 25 to 150 people; the text everywhere else says 35 to 150.","The manuscript uses 2.5×10⁵ neurons per leafcutter worker, while the project's species file lists 4.0×10⁵ for the same species."],"bottom_line":"A careful reader can rely on the broad story (insect castes, seven-neighbour flocking, and layered human societies are all well-cited ideas) and on the 2026 inputs of 6.97×10²⁰ human neurons, 1.2×10²³ bits of storage and 1.89×10²² silicon operations per second. The headline numbers should not be trusted yet: the 280-fold memory law refits to about 127-fold with a looser fit, the hierarchy formula cannot reach the populations it is said to explain, both tables disagree with the project's data file by factors of 10 or more, and every brain-size comparison for insect colonies is off by about a thousandfold.","pdf":"papers/paper5.pdf","corrected_docx":"","pdf_stale":false}];
